# Chapter 3 · Neonatology (owner, 12 Nov 2026: levels and clean wording as in the Obstetrics book).
# Card: (old index, section, question, answer, level). Facts, numbers and doses are unchanged; shorthand is written out in full.
# Dropped: hie 18 (the same calcium gluconate card is neo-metabolic 18).
CARDS = {
'newborn': [
(0, 'Definitions', 'How long is the neonatal period?', 'The first 28 days of life.', 1),
(1, 'Definitions', 'How are preterm, term and post-term births defined?', 'Preterm: before 37 weeks. Term: 37–41 weeks. Post-term: 42 weeks or more.', 1),
(2, 'Definitions', 'How are low, very low and extremely low birth weight defined?', 'Low birth weight: below 2500 g. Very low birth weight: below 1500 g. Extremely low birth weight: below 1000 g.', 1),
(3, 'Definitions', 'How are small and large for gestational age defined?', 'Small for gestational age (SGA): a birth weight below the 10th centile for gestation. Large for gestational age (LGA): above the 90th centile.', 1),
(4, 'Definitions', 'What is the perinatal mortality rate?', 'Stillbirths plus deaths in the first week of life, per 1000 total births.', 2),
(5, 'Normal values', 'What are the normal weight, length and head circumference of a term newborn?', 'Weight 2.5–4 kg, length about 50 cm and head circumference about 35 cm.', 1),
(6, 'Normal values', 'What is the maximum normal weight loss after birth?', 'Up to 10% of the birth weight, regained by 10–14 days.', 1),
(7, 'Normal values', 'What are the normal heart rate and respiratory rate of a newborn?', 'Heart rate 120–160/min and respiratory rate 40–60/min.', 1),
(8, 'Normal values', 'When should a term baby pass meconium and urine?', 'Meconium within 24–48 hours and urine within 24 hours.', 1),
(9, 'Normal values', 'What are the causes of no meconium by 48 hours?', 'Imperforate anus, Hirschsprung disease and meconium ileus (cystic fibrosis).', 2),
(10, 'Examination', 'What are the features of erythema toxicum?', 'Pustules on a red base appear on days 2–3 and contain eosinophils. The baby is well and the rash fades, so the mother is reassured.', 2),
(11, 'Examination', 'What is a Mongolian spot?', 'A blue-grey patch over the lower back and buttocks that fades with time. It is not a bruise.', 1),
(12, 'Examination', 'A newborn girl has breast swelling with milk. What is the next step?', 'Reassure the mother and do not squeeze the breast; the swelling is caused by maternal hormones.', 2),
(13, 'Examination', 'What causes a small vaginal bleed in a newborn girl?', 'Withdrawal of maternal oestrogen. The mother is reassured.', 2),
(14, 'Examination', 'Which skin finding over the lower sacrum is abnormal, and what is done?', 'A sacral dimple or a tuft of hair. A spinal ultrasound is done to look for spina bifida occulta.', 2),
(15, 'Examination', 'A baby has a port-wine stain on the face and later develops seizures. What is the diagnosis?', 'Sturge–Weber syndrome.', 2),
(16, 'Examination', 'How is a strawberry naevus (infantile haemangioma) near the eye treated?', 'With oral propranolol. Most other haemangiomas regress on their own.', 3),
(17, 'Examination', 'What is the clue to bilateral choanal atresia?', 'The baby is cyanosed at rest and during feeding and pink when crying, and a nasogastric tube cannot be passed.', 2),
(18, 'Examination', 'What does an absent red reflex in a newborn indicate?', 'A congenital cataract (or a retinoblastoma). Urgent referral to an eye specialist is needed.', 2),
(19, 'Routine care', 'What routine care is given at birth?', 'Vitamin K 1 mg intramuscularly, eye ointment, BCG, the birth dose of oral polio vaccine and hepatitis B vaccine, early breastfeeding and dry cord care.', 1),
(20, 'Routine care', 'When should breastfeeding start?', 'Within the first hour of birth.', 1),
(21, 'Routine care', 'How is the cord cared for where cord infection is common?', 'Chlorhexidine 7.1% is applied to the cord stump.', 2),
(22, 'Screening', 'What is the screening test for critical congenital heart disease?', 'Pulse oximetry of the right hand and of a foot after 24 hours.', 2),
(23, 'Screening', 'What does heel-prick screening detect?', 'Congenital hypothyroidism, phenylketonuria, sickle cell disease, cystic fibrosis and other disorders.', 1),
(24, 'IMCI', 'What are the IMCI danger signs in a young infant?', 'Not feeding well, convulsions, a respiratory rate of 60/min or more, severe chest indrawing, a temperature of 37.5 °C or more or below 35.5 °C, and movement only when stimulated.', 2),
(25, 'Reflexes', 'Which reflex is NOT present at birth?', 'The parachute reaction, which appears at 8–9 months.', 2),
(26, 'Reflexes', 'What does a Moro reflex absent on both sides mean in a newborn?', 'Depression of the central nervous system: hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy, sepsis, kernicterus or drugs.', 2),
(27, 'Reflexes', 'What does a Moro reflex absent on one side mean?', 'Erb\'s palsy, or a fractured clavicle or humerus.', 2),
],
'nls': [
(0, 'Basics', 'How many newborns need help to breathe at birth?', 'About 1 in 10. Fewer than 1 in 100 need chest compressions or drugs.', 1),
(1, 'Basics', 'What is the single most important step in newborn resuscitation?', 'Effective ventilation of the lungs.', 1),
(2, 'Basics', 'For how long is cord clamping delayed in a well baby?', 'For at least 60 seconds.', 1),
(3, 'Apgar', 'What are the five signs of the Apgar score?', 'Appearance (colour), pulse, grimace (reflex irritability), activity (tone) and respiration.', 1),
(4, 'Apgar', 'When is the Apgar score done?', 'At 1 and 5 minutes, then every 5 minutes during resuscitation.', 1),
(5, 'Apgar', 'Is resuscitation delayed until the 1-minute Apgar score?', 'No. The decision is made on breathing, heart rate and tone.', 2),
(6, 'Steps', 'What is the golden minute?', 'Within the first minute the baby is dried, kept warm, the airway is cleared if needed and the baby is stimulated; if the baby is not breathing, ventilation starts by 60 seconds.', 2),
(7, 'Steps', 'What are the indications for bag-and-mask ventilation?', 'Apnoea, gasping, or a heart rate below 100/min.', 1),
(8, 'Steps', 'How many inflation breaths are given first?', 'Five inflation breaths, followed by ventilation breaths.', 1),
(9, 'Steps', 'What is the ventilation rate in the newborn?', '40–60 breaths per minute.', 1),
(10, 'Steps', 'What oxygen concentration is used to start the resuscitation of a term baby?', 'Room air (21%); 21–30% for babies born before 35 weeks.', 2),
(11, 'Steps', 'What is the best sign of effective ventilation?', 'A rising heart rate, with chest movement.', 2),
(12, 'Steps', 'When are chest compressions started?', 'When the heart rate stays below 60/min after 30 seconds of effective ventilation.', 2),
(13, 'Steps', 'What is the compression-to-ventilation ratio in the newborn?', '3 : 1, which is 90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute.', 1),
(14, 'Steps', 'How are neonatal chest compressions done?', 'With two thumbs on the lower third of the sternum, compressing to one third of the depth of the chest.', 2),
(15, 'Steps', 'A meconium-stained baby is not vigorous. What is the first action?', 'Ventilation is started within the first minute. Routine tracheal suction is not done.', 3),
(16, 'Drugs', 'What is the indication for adrenaline?', 'A heart rate below 60/min despite 30 seconds of good ventilation and chest compressions.', 2),
(17, 'Drugs', 'What is the intravenous dose of adrenaline in the newborn?', '10–30 µg/kg, which is 0.1–0.3 ml/kg of adrenaline 1:10 000, through the umbilical vein.', 2),
(18, 'Drugs', 'What is the fastest vascular access in the delivery room?', 'An umbilical venous catheter.', 1),
(19, 'Drugs', 'Which fluid is given for suspected blood loss, and how much?', '0.9% saline or O-negative blood, 10 ml/kg.', 2),
(20, 'Drugs', 'Is sodium bicarbonate used routinely in newborn resuscitation?', 'No.', 1),
(21, 'Targets', 'What are the target oxygen saturations at 5 and 10 minutes?', '80–85% at 5 minutes and 85–95% at 10 minutes.', 2),
(22, 'After', 'When does the team consider stopping resuscitation?', 'When there is no heart rate after about 20 minutes of full resuscitation.', 2),
(23, 'After', 'How is a baby with a diaphragmatic hernia resuscitated?', 'The baby is intubated at once; bag-and-mask ventilation is avoided, and a nasogastric tube is passed.', 3),
(24, 'After', 'During bagging, a baby suddenly deteriorates and one side of the chest is silent. What is the cause and the treatment?', 'A pneumothorax, treated by needle decompression.', 3),
],
'preterm': [
(0, 'Definitions', 'How is preterm birth graded?', 'Extremely preterm: before 28 weeks. Very preterm: 28 to under 32 weeks. Moderate to late preterm: 32 to under 37 weeks.', 1),
(1, 'Causes', 'What is the commonest cause of preterm birth?', 'Spontaneous preterm labour with no known cause (idiopathic).', 1),
(2, 'Features', 'What are the physical features of a preterm baby?', 'Thin red skin, lanugo, few creases on the soles, soft ears, a small breast nodule and undescended testes.', 1),
(3, 'Features', 'Which score is used to estimate the gestational age after birth?', 'The New Ballard score, which is accurate to ± 2 weeks.', 1),
(4, 'Features', 'When does the coordination of sucking and swallowing mature?', 'At about 34 weeks.', 2),
(5, 'Care', 'At which gestation are antenatal steroids given?', 'At 24–34 weeks, when preterm birth is expected.', 1),
(6, 'Care', 'What is kangaroo mother care?', 'Continuous skin-to-skin contact with the mother, together with breastfeeding, for babies below 2000 g.', 1),
(7, 'Care', 'What is the best milk for preterm babies?', 'The mother\'s own breast milk; donor milk is the next choice.', 1),
(8, 'Care', 'What intravenous fluid is given to a preterm baby on day 1?', '10% glucose 60–80 ml/kg/day, increased by 20 ml/kg/day up to 150 ml/kg/day.', 2),
(9, 'Care', 'Which supplement is started at 2–6 weeks in preterm babies?', 'Iron (and also vitamin D).', 2),
(10, 'Complications', 'What is apnoea of prematurity?', 'A pause in breathing of 20 seconds or more, or a shorter pause with bradycardia or cyanosis.', 1),
(11, 'Complications', 'What is the drug of choice for apnoea of prematurity?', 'Caffeine citrate: a loading dose of 20 mg/kg, then 5–10 mg/kg daily.', 2),
(12, 'Complications', 'What is bronchopulmonary dysplasia?', 'A preterm baby who still needs oxygen at 36 weeks\' corrected age.', 2),
(13, 'Complications', 'When does intraventricular haemorrhage usually occur, and how is it diagnosed?', 'In the first 72 hours of life; it is diagnosed by cranial ultrasound.', 2),
(14, 'Complications', 'What is the late result of periventricular leukomalacia?', 'Spastic diplegia.', 2),
(15, 'Complications', 'What are the signs of a patent ductus arteriosus in a preterm baby?', 'Bounding pulses, a wide pulse pressure, a continuous murmur and heart failure.', 2),
(16, 'Complications', 'How is a patent ductus arteriosus treated medically?', 'With ibuprofen or paracetamol.', 2),
(17, 'Complications', 'Which babies are screened for retinopathy of prematurity?', 'Babies born before 32 weeks or weighing less than 1500 g.', 2),
(18, 'Complications', 'What causes retinopathy of prematurity?', 'Immature retinal vessels together with excess oxygen.', 2),
(19, 'NEC', 'What are the risk factors for necrotising enterocolitis (NEC)?', 'Prematurity, formula feeds, a fast increase in feeds, asphyxia, polycythaemia and a patent ductus arteriosus.', 2),
(20, 'NEC', 'What are the clinical features of NEC?', 'Feed intolerance, bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, bloody stools and shock.', 2),
(21, 'NEC', 'What is the typical X-ray sign of NEC?', 'Pneumatosis intestinalis (gas in the wall of the bowel).', 1),
(22, 'NEC', 'How is NEC treated?', 'Nil by mouth, nasogastric drainage, intravenous fluids, and ampicillin, gentamicin and metronidazole; surgery if the bowel perforates.', 2),
(23, 'IUGR', 'How does symmetrical intrauterine growth restriction differ from asymmetrical?', 'Symmetrical growth restriction starts early and all measurements are small (chromosomal disorders, congenital infections). Asymmetrical growth restriction starts late and the head is spared (placental cause).', 3),
(24, 'IUGR', 'Which type of intrauterine growth restriction has the better prognosis?', 'Asymmetrical growth restriction, because the head is spared.', 2),
(25, 'IUGR', 'What are the problems of a baby who is small for gestational age?', 'Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, polycythaemia, asphyxia and hypocalcaemia.', 2),
(26, 'Large and post-term', 'What is the main cause of babies who are large for gestational age?', 'Maternal diabetes. Other causes are large parents and Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome.', 1),
(27, 'Large and post-term', 'What are the features of a post-term baby?', 'The baby is alert, with dry peeling skin, long nails and meconium staining.', 1),
(28, 'Large and post-term', 'What is the main risk for a post-term baby?', 'Meconium aspiration and asphyxia.', 2),
],
'neo-rds': [
(0, 'Signs', 'What are the signs of respiratory distress in a newborn?', 'A respiratory rate above 60/min, grunting, nasal flaring, chest recession and cyanosis.', 1),
(1, 'Causes', 'What is the commonest cause of respiratory distress in a term baby?', 'Transient tachypnoea of the newborn.', 1),
(2, 'Causes', 'What is the commonest cause of respiratory distress in a preterm baby?', 'Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS).', 1),
(3, 'RDS', 'What causes respiratory distress syndrome?', 'Deficiency of surfactant, which is made by type II pneumocytes and matures at 35 weeks.', 1),
(4, 'RDS', 'Which factors increase the risk of RDS?', 'Prematurity, a mother with diabetes, male sex, being the second twin, elective caesarean section and asphyxia.', 2),
(5, 'RDS', 'Which factors decrease the risk of RDS?', 'Antenatal steroids, prolonged rupture of the membranes, intrauterine growth restriction and maternal hypertension.', 3),
(6, 'RDS', 'When do the symptoms of RDS appear, and how do they progress?', 'Within 4 hours of birth; they worsen for 48–72 hours and then improve.', 2),
(7, 'RDS', 'What does the chest X-ray show in RDS?', 'Diffuse ground-glass lungs with air bronchograms and a small lung volume.', 1),
(8, 'RDS', 'What is the first respiratory support in RDS?', 'Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) of 6–8 cm H₂O.', 2),
(9, 'RDS', 'When is surfactant given?', 'When the oxygen need rises above 30% on CPAP, or when the baby is ventilated.', 2),
(10, 'RDS', 'What is the first dose of poractant (surfactant)?', '200 mg/kg.', 2),
(11, 'RDS', 'What is the oxygen saturation target in preterm babies?', '90–94%.', 2),
(12, 'RDS', 'Why are antibiotics given in RDS?', 'Pneumonia and sepsis look the same as RDS. Antibiotics are stopped if the cultures are negative.', 3),
(13, 'TTN', 'What causes transient tachypnoea of the newborn, and in which baby is it seen?', 'Slow clearance of lung fluid, in a term baby born by elective caesarean section.', 2),
(14, 'TTN', 'What does the X-ray show in transient tachypnoea of the newborn?', 'Fluid in the horizontal fissure and streaky markings at the hila.', 2),
(15, 'TTN', 'How long does transient tachypnoea of the newborn last?', '24–48 hours.', 1),
(16, 'MAS', 'In which baby is meconium aspiration syndrome seen?', 'A term or post-term baby with fetal distress.', 1),
(17, 'MAS', 'What does the X-ray show in meconium aspiration syndrome?', 'Hyperinflation with patchy collapse and consolidation.', 2),
(18, 'MAS', 'What are the main complications of meconium aspiration syndrome?', 'Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN) and pneumothorax.', 2),
(19, 'Surgical', 'What are the signs of a left diaphragmatic hernia?', 'Respiratory distress at birth, a scaphoid abdomen, heart sounds on the right and bowel sounds in the chest.', 2),
(20, 'Surgical', 'What are the first steps in a diaphragmatic hernia?', 'Intubation (no bag-and-mask ventilation) and a nasogastric tube.', 2),
(21, 'Surgical', 'What decides survival in congenital diaphragmatic hernia?', 'The degree of pulmonary hypoplasia.', 3),
(22, 'Surgical', 'What is the clue to oesophageal atresia?', 'Frothy saliva, choking at the first feed, and a nasogastric tube that coils on the X-ray.', 2),
(23, 'Pneumothorax', 'What is the emergency treatment of a tension pneumothorax?', 'Needle aspiration in the second intercostal space, followed by a chest drain.', 2),
(24, 'Cyanosis', 'How does central cyanosis differ from peripheral cyanosis?', 'Central cyanosis is a blue tongue and is always abnormal. Peripheral cyanosis is blue hands and feet with a pink tongue.', 2),
(25, 'Cyanosis', 'How is the hyperoxia test interpreted?', 'If the PaO₂ rises above 150 mmHg in 100% oxygen, the cause is in the lungs; if it stays low, the cause is cyanotic heart disease.', 3),
(26, 'Cyanosis', 'Which drug keeps the ductus arteriosus open?', 'Prostaglandin E1 infusion, 5–10 ng/kg/min.', 2),
(27, 'Cyanosis', 'What is the main side effect of prostaglandin E1?', 'Apnoea.', 2),
(28, 'Cyanosis', 'How is persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn treated?', 'With ventilation and inhaled nitric oxide.', 2),
(29, 'Apnoea', 'What are the causes of apnoea in a term newborn?', 'Sepsis, meningitis, seizures, hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and intraventricular haemorrhage.', 2),
],
'neo-jaundice': [
(0, 'Basics', 'At what bilirubin level is jaundice visible in the newborn?', 'At about 5 mg/dL (85 µmol/L).', 1),
(1, 'Basics', 'Why do newborns become jaundiced?', 'A high red cell mass, a short red cell life, immature glucuronyl transferase and the enterohepatic circulation of bilirubin.', 2),
(2, 'Physiological', 'When does physiological jaundice start?', 'After 24 hours of age (on day 2–3).', 1),
(3, 'Physiological', 'When does physiological jaundice peak in term babies, and how high?', 'On day 3–4, at no more than 12 mg/dL.', 1),
(4, 'Physiological', 'Can physiological jaundice cause kernicterus?', 'No, not in a healthy term baby.', 2),
(5, 'Pathological', 'What are the signs that neonatal jaundice is pathological?', 'Onset in the first 24 hours, a rise of more than 5 mg/dL a day, a level above the phototherapy line, conjugated jaundice, jaundice lasting more than 14 days, or an ill baby.', 2),
(6, 'Pathological', 'What are the causes of jaundice in the first 24 hours?', 'Rhesus or ABO incompatibility, G6PD deficiency, hereditary spherocytosis, congenital infection and sepsis.', 2),
(7, 'Pathological', 'How is prolonged jaundice defined?', 'Jaundice lasting more than 14 days in a term baby or more than 21 days in a preterm baby.', 1),
(8, 'Pathological', 'What are the causes of prolonged unconjugated jaundice?', 'Breast milk jaundice, hypothyroidism, urinary tract infection, haemolysis and Crigler–Najjar syndrome.', 2),
(9, 'Haemolysis', 'What are the blood groups of mother and baby in rhesus disease?', 'A rhesus-negative mother and a rhesus-positive baby.', 1),
(10, 'Haemolysis', 'Why is the first baby usually spared in rhesus disease?', 'The mother is usually sensitised at the first delivery.', 2),
(11, 'Haemolysis', 'How is rhesus isoimmunisation prevented?', 'Anti-D is given to rhesus-negative mothers at 28 weeks and within 72 hours of delivery.', 2),
(12, 'Haemolysis', 'What are the blood groups in ABO incompatibility?', 'The mother is group O and the baby is group A or B.', 1),
(13, 'Haemolysis', 'How does ABO incompatibility differ from rhesus disease?', 'ABO incompatibility is commoner and milder, affects the first baby and gives a weak Coombs test. Rhesus disease is severe, causes hydrops and gives a strongly positive Coombs test.', 3),
(14, 'Haemolysis', 'What is the key test in suspected haemolytic jaundice?', 'The blood groups of mother and baby, and a direct Coombs test.', 2),
(15, 'Breast milk', 'How does breastfeeding jaundice differ from breast milk jaundice?', 'Breastfeeding jaundice occurs in the first week because the baby gets too little milk. Breast milk jaundice occurs after the first week in a thriving baby and is caused by a factor in the milk.', 3),
(16, 'Breast milk', 'How is breast milk jaundice managed?', 'Breastfeeding continues once other causes have been excluded.', 2),
(17, 'Conjugated', 'How is conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia defined?', 'A direct bilirubin above 1–2 mg/dL or above 20% of the total bilirubin.', 2),
(18, 'Conjugated', 'What is the clinical clue to biliary atresia?', 'Prolonged jaundice with pale stools and dark urine in a well baby.', 2),
(19, 'Conjugated', 'How is biliary atresia treated?', 'With the Kasai portoenterostomy before 60 days of age.', 2),
(20, 'Conjugated', 'Which metabolic disease causes conjugated jaundice in a newborn?', 'Galactosaemia; α1-antitrypsin deficiency is another cause.', 2),
(21, 'Phototherapy', 'How does phototherapy work?', 'Blue light (460–490 nm) converts bilirubin into water-soluble isomers that are excreted.', 2),
(22, 'Phototherapy', 'How is the need for phototherapy decided?', 'The bilirubin is plotted on the hour-specific chart for the baby\'s gestation and risk factors.', 2),
(23, 'Phototherapy', 'What are the side effects of phototherapy?', 'Dehydration, loose stools, a rash and an unstable temperature.', 1),
(24, 'Phototherapy', 'What is the contraindication to phototherapy?', 'Conjugated jaundice, which causes the bronze baby syndrome.', 3),
(25, 'Exchange', 'What are the indications for exchange transfusion?', 'A bilirubin above the exchange line, signs of bilirubin encephalopathy, or severe haemolytic anaemia.', 2),
(26, 'Exchange', 'What volume and which blood are used for exchange transfusion?', 'A double volume (2 × 80–90 ml/kg) of group O rhesus-negative blood.', 2),
(27, 'Exchange', 'What are the complications of exchange transfusion?', 'Hypocalcaemia, hypoglycaemia, hyperkalaemia, thrombocytopenia, infection and necrotising enterocolitis.', 2),
(28, 'Exchange', 'What is the dose of intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) in isoimmune haemolysis?', '0.5–1 g/kg over 2 hours.', 2),
(29, 'Kernicterus', 'Where is bilirubin deposited in kernicterus?', 'In the basal ganglia and the nuclei of the brainstem.', 1),
(30, 'Kernicterus', 'What are the earliest signs of bilirubin encephalopathy?', 'Lethargy and poor feeding, followed by a high-pitched cry and arching.', 2),
(31, 'Kernicterus', 'What are the long-term effects of kernicterus?', 'Choreoathetoid cerebral palsy, sensorineural deafness, paralysis of upward gaze and defects of the tooth enamel.', 2),
(32, 'Kernicterus', 'Which drugs displace bilirubin from albumin?', 'Sulphonamides and ceftriaxone.', 3),
],
}

CARDS.update({
'neo-sepsis': [
(0, 'Sepsis', 'What age separates early-onset from late-onset neonatal sepsis?', '72 hours of age.', 1),
(1, 'Sepsis', 'What are the risk factors for early-onset sepsis?', 'Rupture of the membranes for more than 18 hours, maternal fever or chorioamnionitis, maternal group B streptococcus, and preterm labour.', 2),
(2, 'Sepsis', 'Which organisms cause early-onset sepsis?', 'Group B streptococcus, E. coli, Klebsiella and Listeria.', 2),
(3, 'Sepsis', 'Which organisms cause late-onset (hospital-acquired) sepsis?', 'Staphylococcus aureus, coagulase-negative staphylococci, Klebsiella and Pseudomonas.', 2),
(4, 'Sepsis', 'What is the commonest cause of early neonatal sepsis and meningitis?', 'Group B streptococcus.', 1),
(5, 'Sepsis', 'What are the clinical features of neonatal sepsis?', 'Poor feeding, lethargy, an unstable temperature, respiratory distress, apnoea, jaundice and poor perfusion.', 1),
(6, 'Sepsis', 'What are the signs of meningitis in a newborn?', 'A bulging fontanelle, seizures and a high-pitched cry. Neck stiffness is rare.', 2),
(7, 'Sepsis', 'What is the best confirmatory test for neonatal sepsis?', 'A blood culture.', 1),
(8, 'Sepsis', 'Which newborns should have a lumbar puncture?', 'Every newborn with suspected sepsis who is stable enough.', 2),
(9, 'Treatment', 'What are the first-line antibiotics for neonatal sepsis?', 'Ampicillin and gentamicin for at least 10 days (WHO).', 1),
(10, 'Treatment', 'What is the dose of ampicillin in the first week of life?', '50 mg/kg every 12 hours (every 8 hours after the first week).', 2),
(11, 'Treatment', 'What is the dose of gentamicin in the first week of life?', '5 mg/kg once daily (7.5 mg/kg after the first week).', 2),
(12, 'Treatment', 'Which antibiotics are used for neonatal meningitis?', 'Cefotaxime with ampicillin or gentamicin, for at least 3 weeks.', 2),
(13, 'Treatment', 'Why is ampicillin needed in neonatal meningitis?', 'Listeria is resistant to cephalosporins.', 3),
(14, 'Treatment', 'Which antibiotics are used for omphalitis or sepsis from a skin source?', 'Cloxacillin and gentamicin.', 2),
(15, 'Treatment', 'How is early group B streptococcal sepsis prevented?', 'Intrapartum penicillin for mothers who carry group B streptococcus or are at high risk.', 2),
(16, 'TORCH', 'What are the common features of congenital (TORCH) infections?', 'Intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, petechiae, chorioretinitis and deafness.', 2),
(17, 'TORCH', 'What is the triad of congenital toxoplasmosis?', 'Hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis and diffuse intracranial calcification.', 1),
(18, 'TORCH', 'How is congenital toxoplasmosis treated?', 'Pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine, with folinic acid, for 1 year.', 2),
(19, 'TORCH', 'What is the triad of congenital rubella?', 'Cataract, sensorineural deafness and patent ductus arteriosus.', 1),
(20, 'TORCH', 'What is the commonest congenital infection?', 'Cytomegalovirus (CMV).', 1),
(21, 'TORCH', 'What is the pattern of intracranial calcification in congenital CMV infection?', 'Periventricular calcification.', 2),
(22, 'TORCH', 'What is the commonest infectious cause of congenital deafness?', 'Cytomegalovirus.', 2),
(23, 'TORCH', 'What are the early signs of congenital syphilis?', 'Snuffles, a rash on the palms and soles, hepatosplenomegaly and pseudoparalysis.', 2),
(24, 'TORCH', 'What are the late signs of congenital syphilis?', 'Hutchinson teeth, a saddle nose, sabre shins and interstitial keratitis.', 2),
(25, 'TORCH', 'How is neonatal herpes simplex infection treated?', 'With intravenous aciclovir.', 1),
(26, 'TORCH', 'What does the baby of an HBsAg-positive mother receive?', 'Hepatitis B vaccine within 24 hours of birth, together with hepatitis B immunoglobulin.', 2),
(27, 'Local', 'When does gonococcal conjunctivitis start, and how is it treated?', 'On days 2–5. It is treated with ceftriaxone 50 mg/kg intramuscularly once (maximum 125 mg).', 2),
(28, 'Local', 'When does chlamydial conjunctivitis start, and how is it treated?', 'On days 5–14. It is treated with oral erythromycin for 14 days.', 2),
(29, 'Local', 'How is oral thrush treated?', 'With nystatin drops; the mother\'s nipples are treated as well.', 1),
(30, 'Tetanus', 'What causes neonatal tetanus?', 'Clostridium tetani entering through unclean cord care, in the baby of an unvaccinated mother.', 1),
(31, 'Tetanus', 'What is the first symptom of neonatal tetanus?', 'A baby who sucked well stops sucking, because of trismus.', 2),
(32, 'Tetanus', 'How is neonatal tetanus treated?', 'A quiet room, tetanus immunoglobulin, metronidazole, diazepam and feeding by nasogastric tube.', 2),
(33, 'Tetanus', 'How is neonatal tetanus prevented?', 'Td vaccination of women, with clean delivery and clean cord care.', 1),
],
'hie': [
(0, 'HIE', 'What is perinatal asphyxia?', 'Failure of gas exchange around the time of birth, causing hypoxaemia, hypercapnia and acidosis.', 1),
(1, 'HIE', 'What are the criteria of asphyxia that causes hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy (HIE)?', 'A cord pH below 7.0 (base deficit of 12 or more), a low Apgar score at 10 minutes, early encephalopathy and dysfunction of several organs.', 2),
(2, 'HIE', 'When does the asphyxial insult most commonly occur?', 'Before or during labour (in about 90% of cases).', 2),
(3, 'HIE', 'What are the features of Sarnat stage 1 HIE?', 'The baby is hyperalert with normal tone and no seizures; the outcome is good.', 2),
(4, 'HIE', 'What are the features of Sarnat stage 2 HIE?', 'The baby is lethargic and hypotonic with weak reflexes, and seizures are common.', 2),
(5, 'HIE', 'What are the features of Sarnat stage 3 HIE?', 'Coma, flaccidity, absent reflexes and frequent seizures; the outcome is poor.', 2),
(6, 'HIE', 'What kidney problems follow asphyxia?', 'Acute tubular necrosis, oliguria and inappropriate ADH secretion (hyponatraemia).', 2),
(7, 'HIE', 'How are fluids managed after asphyxia?', 'Fluids are restricted, to about 40–60 ml/kg/day on day 1.', 2),
(8, 'HIE', 'Why is hyperthermia avoided in HIE?', 'Every degree above normal adds to the brain injury.', 3),
(9, 'Cooling', 'What are the criteria for therapeutic hypothermia?', 'A baby of 36 weeks or more, with asphyxia and moderate or severe HIE, within 6 hours of birth.', 2),
(10, 'Cooling', 'What are the target temperature and duration of cooling?', 'A core temperature of 33.5 °C for 72 hours.', 2),
(11, 'Cooling', 'Is mild HIE treated with cooling?', 'No.', 2),
(12, 'HIE', 'What are the bad prognostic signs in HIE?', 'A severe stage, seizures lasting more than 24 hours, a burst-suppression EEG and injury of the basal ganglia on MRI.', 3),
(13, 'Seizures', 'What is the commonest cause of neonatal seizures?', 'Hypoxic–ischaemic encephalopathy.', 1),
(14, 'Seizures', 'What is the commonest type of neonatal seizure?', 'Subtle seizures: lip smacking, eye deviation, cycling movements of the limbs and apnoea.', 2),
(15, 'Seizures', 'How is jitteriness told apart from a seizure?', 'Jitteriness is provoked by stimulation and stops when the limb is held, and there are no eye signs or apnoea.', 3),
(16, 'Seizures', 'What is the first investigation in a newborn with seizures?', 'The blood glucose.', 1),
(17, 'Seizures', 'What dose of 10% glucose is given for hypoglycaemic seizures?', '2 ml/kg intravenously, followed by an infusion.', 2),
(19, 'Seizures', 'What is the drug of choice for neonatal seizures?', 'Phenobarbital, with an intravenous loading dose of 20 mg/kg.', 1),
(20, 'Seizures', 'What are the second-line drugs for neonatal seizures?', 'Phenytoin, levetiracetam or midazolam.', 2),
(21, 'Seizures', 'What is tried for seizures that resist the usual drugs?', 'A trial of pyridoxine 100 mg intravenously.', 3),
(22, 'Seizures', 'When are anticonvulsants stopped after acute neonatal seizures?', 'Before discharge, once the seizures have stopped.', 2),
(23, 'Seizures', 'What do seizures that do not respond to calcium suggest?', 'Hypomagnesaemia.', 3),
],
'neo-metabolic': [
(0, 'IDM', 'What is the mechanism of the complications in an infant of a diabetic mother?', 'Maternal hyperglycaemia causes fetal hyperglycaemia, which causes fetal hyperinsulinism.', 2),
(1, 'IDM', 'Why are infants of diabetic mothers macrosomic?', 'Fetal insulin acts as a growth hormone.', 2),
(2, 'IDM', 'When does hypoglycaemia occur in an infant of a diabetic mother?', 'In the first hours of life.', 1),
(3, 'IDM', 'Why is an infant of a diabetic mother at risk of respiratory distress syndrome?', 'Insulin delays the production of surfactant.', 2),
(4, 'IDM', 'What causes hypocalcaemia in an infant of a diabetic mother?', 'Transient hypoparathyroidism, on days 1–3.', 2),
(5, 'IDM', 'Why do infants of diabetic mothers develop polycythaemia?', 'Fetal hypoxia increases erythropoietin.', 2),
(6, 'IDM', 'Which heart muscle problem occurs in an infant of a diabetic mother?', 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with a thick septum, which usually resolves.', 2),
(7, 'IDM', 'What is the most specific congenital anomaly of an infant of a diabetic mother?', 'Caudal regression (sacral agenesis).', 2),
(8, 'IDM', 'Which bowel anomaly occurs in infants of diabetic mothers?', 'Small left colon syndrome.', 2),
(9, 'IDM', 'Which birth injuries occur in a macrosomic infant of a diabetic mother?', 'Shoulder dystocia, Erb\'s palsy and a fractured clavicle.', 1),
(10, 'Hypoglycaemia', 'At what glucose level is a newborn treated for hypoglycaemia?', 'Below 2.6 mmol/L (47 mg/dL), or any low value with symptoms.', 1),
(11, 'Hypoglycaemia', 'Which newborns are at risk of hypoglycaemia?', 'Infants of diabetic mothers; babies who are small for gestational age, growth-restricted, preterm or large for gestational age; and babies with sepsis, hypothermia or asphyxia.', 2),
(12, 'Hypoglycaemia', 'What are the symptoms of neonatal hypoglycaemia?', 'Jitteriness, poor feeding, lethargy, apnoea, cyanosis and seizures; often there are no symptoms.', 1),
(13, 'Hypoglycaemia', 'How is asymptomatic neonatal hypoglycaemia treated?', 'A feed and 40% dextrose gel 0.5 ml/kg into the cheek, then the glucose is rechecked.', 2),
(14, 'Hypoglycaemia', 'How is symptomatic neonatal hypoglycaemia treated?', '10% glucose 2 ml/kg intravenously, followed by an infusion of 60–90 ml/kg/day.', 2),
(15, 'Hypoglycaemia', 'A newborn needs more than 8 mg/kg/min of glucose to stay normoglycaemic. What is the likely cause and treatment?', 'Hyperinsulinism, treated with diazoxide. Insulin and cortisol levels are checked.', 3),
(16, 'Hypocalcaemia', 'What are the causes of early neonatal hypocalcaemia?', 'Prematurity, a diabetic mother, asphyxia and intrauterine growth restriction.', 2),
(17, 'Hypocalcaemia', 'What are the causes of late neonatal hypocalcaemia?', 'Cow\'s milk (high phosphate), hypoparathyroidism (DiGeorge syndrome), low magnesium and vitamin D deficiency.', 2),
(18, 'Hypocalcaemia', 'How is symptomatic neonatal hypocalcaemia (or a hypocalcaemic seizure) treated?', '10% calcium gluconate 2 ml/kg intravenously over 10 minutes, with ECG monitoring.', 2),
(19, 'Polycythaemia', 'How is neonatal polycythaemia defined?', 'A venous haematocrit above 65%.', 1),
(20, 'Polycythaemia', 'How is symptomatic polycythaemia treated?', 'With a partial exchange transfusion using 0.9% saline.', 2),
(21, 'Anaemia', 'When is the haemoglobin lowest in the physiological anaemia of infancy?', 'At 8–12 weeks, at about 10–11 g/dL.', 1),
(22, 'VKDB', 'Which clotting factors depend on vitamin K?', 'Factors II, VII, IX and X.', 1),
(23, 'VKDB', 'When does classic vitamin K deficiency bleeding occur, and why?', 'On days 2–7, in a baby who did not receive vitamin K at birth and is exclusively breastfed.', 2),
(24, 'VKDB', 'What causes early vitamin K deficiency bleeding (in the first 24 hours)?', 'Maternal anticonvulsants, warfarin or rifampicin.', 3),
(25, 'VKDB', 'What causes late vitamin K deficiency bleeding?', 'Cholestasis or malabsorption, at 2–12 weeks; it often presents as an intracranial bleed.', 3),
(26, 'VKDB', 'What are the laboratory findings in vitamin K deficiency bleeding?', 'A prolonged prothrombin time (and APTT) with normal platelets.', 2),
(27, 'VKDB', 'How is vitamin K deficiency bleeding treated?', 'Vitamin K 1 mg intravenously, with fresh frozen plasma 10–15 ml/kg if the bleeding is serious.', 2),
(28, 'VKDB', 'Which test shows that blood in a newborn\'s vomit or stool is swallowed maternal blood?', 'The Apt (alkali denaturation) test: fetal haemoglobin stays pink, while maternal blood turns brown.', 3),
],
'birth-injury': [
(0, 'Risk', 'What are the risk factors for birth injury?', 'Macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, breech presentation, instrumental delivery and prolonged labour.', 1),
(1, 'Scalp', 'What are the features of caput succedaneum?', 'Oedema of the scalp present at birth; it crosses the suture lines and resolves in a few days.', 1),
(2, 'Scalp', 'What are the features of a cephalhaematoma?', 'Blood under the periosteum that appears after some hours, is limited to one bone and resolves in weeks.', 2),
(3, 'Scalp', 'What are the complications of a cephalhaematoma?', 'Jaundice, anaemia and a linear skull fracture.', 2),
(4, 'Scalp', 'Should a cephalhaematoma be aspirated?', 'No, because of the risk of infection.', 2),
(5, 'Scalp', 'What is the most dangerous scalp swelling of the newborn?', 'Subgaleal haemorrhage, which can cause shock and disseminated intravascular coagulation; it often follows vacuum delivery.', 3),
(6, 'Nerves', 'Which nerve roots are injured in Erb\'s palsy?', 'C5–C6.', 1),
(7, 'Nerves', 'What is the posture in Erb\'s palsy?', 'The "waiter\'s tip" posture: the arm adducted and internally rotated, the forearm pronated and the wrist flexed.', 2),
(8, 'Nerves', 'What are the reflexes in Erb\'s palsy?', 'The Moro reflex is absent on that side, and the grasp reflex is present.', 2),
(9, 'Nerves', 'Which roots are injured in Klumpke\'s palsy, and what is the posture?', 'C8–T1: a claw hand with an absent grasp reflex.', 2),
(10, 'Nerves', 'What may accompany Klumpke\'s palsy?', 'Horner syndrome, from injury of T1.', 3),
(11, 'Nerves', 'How is brachial plexus palsy managed?', 'With gentle physiotherapy; most babies recover by 3 months.', 2),
(12, 'Nerves', 'Which nerve injury may accompany Erb\'s palsy and cause respiratory distress?', 'Injury of the phrenic nerve (C3–C5), which causes a raised hemidiaphragm.', 3),
(13, 'Nerves', 'What are the clue to and the care of neonatal facial palsy?', 'It follows forceps delivery; the eye does not close and the mouth is pulled to the normal side. Artificial tears protect the eye.', 2),
(14, 'Bones', 'What is the commonest fracture at birth?', 'A fracture of the clavicle.', 1),
(15, 'Bones', 'How is a fractured clavicle treated in a newborn?', 'With gentle handling (pinning the sleeve); it heals on its own.', 1),
(16, 'Soft tissue', 'What are the features and treatment of a sternomastoid tumour?', 'A firm lump in the neck at 2–3 weeks that causes torticollis. It is treated with stretching physiotherapy.', 2),
(17, 'DDH', 'What are the risk factors for developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)?', 'Female sex, breech presentation, being first-born, a family history and oligohydramnios.', 1),
(18, 'DDH', 'How do the Barlow and Ortolani tests differ?', 'The Barlow test dislocates an unstable hip; the Ortolani test relocates a dislocated hip with a clunk.', 2),
(19, 'DDH', 'What are the later signs of DDH?', 'Limited abduction, asymmetrical skin creases, the Galeazzi sign and a limp.', 2),
(20, 'DDH', 'Which investigation is used for DDH before 4–6 months of age?', 'A hip ultrasound.', 2),
(21, 'DDH', 'How is DDH treated under 6 months of age?', 'With a Pavlik harness.', 1),
(22, 'Club foot', 'What are the features of club foot?', 'The foot is fixed in equinus and varus and cannot be corrected passively.', 1),
(23, 'Club foot', 'How does positional talipes differ from club foot?', 'Positional talipes corrects fully with gentle pressure; club foot is fixed.', 2),
(24, 'Club foot', 'How is club foot treated?', 'With Ponseti serial casting, tenotomy and an abduction brace.', 2),
],
})

